import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("You see a door with three signs. Which one do you choose?");
        System.out.println("1. Red sign");
        System.out.println("2. Blue sign");
        System.out.println("3. Green sign");

        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("You chose the red sign. Congratulations! You won the game.");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("You chose the blue sign. Unfortunately, you lost the game.");
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("You chose the green sign. You are still playing the game.");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid choice. Please choose a number between 1 and 3.");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the player is presented with three choices, and their selection is handled using a switch statement. You can customize the dialogue and the outcomes for each choice to fit your game's story and mechanics.

